\ ==============================================================================
\ neuron.tatatina
\ Irundykue-syry : Singular Neuron, Forward Pass
\ ==============================================================================
\
\ Status
\   Forward pass only; no error backpropagation is used. This is a test
\   program for the dialect, not a verified model. The Idris front end does not
\   model >R, R>, * or 2DROP yet, and no theorem of the upstream Agda notes is
\   applied to this file. The expected output is in test/neuron.expected, and
\   the test is listed in test/XFAIL until the interpreter exists.
\
\ Open questions of this project (not established by the upstream notes)
\   Q1. Can the second-operator lemmas serve, at the singular point A/0, in the
\       way a derived-category treatment (D-module-like) would: a recovery
\       value that is defined exactly where the primary path is undefined?
\   Q2. If so, is it legitimate to let them? What must a recovery value
\       preserve for that, and how would this be tested?
\   Only one part touches the upstream notes: Ext^1 is a derived-functor object,
\   and the notes realise it as the cokernel of a coboundary map. They contain
\   nothing about D-modules, differential operators or learning rules.
\
\ Relation to the upstream notes (an analogy, not a derivation)
\   Upstream (AC2, AC3, AC6): a coarse map U loses information, and a second
\   map S recovers exactly what U loses. S is unchanged by the lost "noise"
\   (S-ext-invariant-under-all-coboundaries) and changes only with it
\   (S-ext-complete). This is proved for specific instances only, and no
\   uniform S is claimed.
\   Differences that matter here:
\   - Upstream applies S after U. CO-EXECUTE runs the recovery path instead of
\     the primary path, after the primary path has thrown.
\   - The upstream pair L_u, L_v (AC10) are anticommuting left-multiplication
\     matrices. The words below are neither matrices nor anticommuting, so the
\     names L_u and L_v are not used in this file.
\   - ext1 below is the THROW code -10, a label used as a number. An upstream
\     Ext^1 class is a cokernel class, and extractor-separates shows that the
\     upstream extractor is not constant.
\   - The Abelian Curse of the notes is the information lost by an
\     abelianization. A division by zero is a different failure: it has no
\     result at all.

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\ 1. Primary Path and Recovery Path (Strict Stack Semantics)
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\ Primary path: computes x / w_u.
\ A zero w_u is the singular point. The / word throws -10 (division by zero).
\ The AArch64 SDIV instruction does not trap, so / must test the divisor itself.
\ Stack: ( x w_u w_v -- y )
: MAC-NORMAL
  DROP                  \ Discard w_v -> ( x w_u )
  /                     \ x / w_u; throws -10 if w_u = 0
;

\ Recovery path: runs instead of MAC-NORMAL when MAC-NORMAL throws -10.
\ CO-EXECUTE first restores the stacks to their state before MAC-NORMAL, then
\ pushes the THROW code -10 on TOP of the data stack. The value is the same as
\ tokenValue DivByZeroResidue in Irundykue.Exception.Taxonomy.
\ -10 is a label used as a number, so the result depends on the numbering that
\ Forth 2012 assigns to division by zero.
\ The result does not depend on x or w_u. What counts as noise that a recovery
\ value must be invariant under is not defined yet, so neither the invariance
\ nor the completeness of the upstream notes is claimed.
\ Stack: ( x w_u w_v ext1 -- y' )
: EXT1-ACTIVATION
  *                     \ ext1 * w_v -> ( x w_u [ext1*w_v] )
  >R 2DROP R>           \ Discard x and w_u -> ( [ext1*w_v] )
;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\ 2. The Singular Neuron Definition
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

\ Forward pass of the neuron: y is x / w_u, or the recovery value when w_u = 0,
\ and the output is y + bias.
\ Stack: ( x w_u w_v bias -- output )
: SINGULAR-NEURON
  \ Step 1: Scalar execution of both paths, before the orthogonal subspace.
  >R                    \ Save bias to the return stack -> ( x w_u w_v )
  ['] MAC-NORMAL ['] EXT1-ACTIVATION CO-EXECUTE
                        \ Scalar y, from either path -> ( y )
  R>                    \ Retrieve bias -> ( y bias )

  \ Step 2: Pack four values into the 4-slot layout.
  \ The layout stands for the basis (1, X, Y, XY) of Cl_N(2), whose dimension
  \ is 4. No Clifford product is used yet.
  0 0                   \ -> ( y bias 0 0 )

  \ Step 3: Move the four values to the orthogonal stack.
  \ Consumes exactly 4 items; the data stack is empty afterwards.
  >ORTHO

  \ Step 4: Transaction and contraction.
  \ [H and ]H are no-ops under QEMU, which has no FEAT_TME. The Idris front end
  \ only checks that they are not nested.
  \ CL-CONTRACT does not change the stack. The contraction it stands for is not
  \ defined yet.
  [H
    CL-CONTRACT
  ]H

  \ Step 5: Move the values back and add the bias.
  ORTHO>                \ Restores ( y bias 0 0 )
  2DROP +               \ Discard the two zero slots, then y + bias -> ( output )
;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\ 3. Execution Stream and Deterministic Test Harness
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

: RUN-TESTS
  \ Test Case 1: Non-Singular Path
  \ Inputs: x=10, w_u=2, w_v=5, bias=1
  \ Expected: 10 / 2 = 5, then 5 + 1 = 6
  10 2 5 1 SINGULAR-NEURON
  ." Test 1 (Stable Path): " . CR

  \ Test Case 2: Singular Path (recovery value)
  \ Inputs: x=10, w_u=0, w_v=5, bias=1
  \ 10 / 0 throws -10, so EXT1-ACTIVATION runs with ext1 = -10.
  \ Expected: -10 * 5 = -50, then -50 + 1 = -49
  10 0 5 1 SINGULAR-NEURON
  ." Test 2 (Singular Spike): " . CR
;

\ Run the tests
RUN-TESTS
